@*
    The loading screen — the honesty screen.

    NOT SETUP. Setup is the one-time "do you want to spend 800 MB" decision.
    This is what somebody meets on EVERY launch, and its job is the one Aether's
    opening screen does: while the phone gets itself ready, tell the person
    exactly what this handset can and cannot do, in plain words, so that every
    limitation they hit later was disclosed at the door rather than discovered
    as a fault.

    THREE THINGS AT ONCE.
      - What this phone can already do, and what it is still missing. Named, every
        row, because a count of something the screen does not show is Aether's one
        mistake - "10 of 10 ready" over a list of seven.
      - Work started behind it. Anything missing begins downloading here; the
        person does not press a button to make their phone useful.
      - It gets OUT OF THE WAY. On the fifth launch everything is present, the
        census is all ticks, and this is a half-second receipt on the way to Home.

    IT DOES NOT HOLD YOU UNTIL THE LAST BYTE. This screen used to sit on a fresh
    install for the whole of ~800 MB - full screen, no bottom bar, no circle, no
    tour - because it awaited the entire plan before navigating. There was no way
    out of it and nothing to do in it.

    The plan is ordered so the phone becomes useful soonest: the voice and the
    ears come before the brain. The moment those two have landed the phone can
    hold a conversation, and that is the moment this screen leaves. The download
    is not cancelled and not restarted - ISetup owns the run, RunAsync attaches
    to one already in flight, and IsRunning exists for exactly this. Home shows
    the rest of it coming down with the bar under it.
*@
@page "/"
@inject ISetup Wizard
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IConversation Talk
@inject IWiringProbe Probe
@* THE BRAIN, because warming the voice and leaving the model cold is half a job. *@
@inject IBrain Brain
@implements IDisposable

<div class="loading">

    <div class="loading-mark">
        <BrandMark Fluid="true" State="MarkState.Thinking" Halo="true" Ring="true" Decorative="true" />
    </div>

    <h1 class="loading-lead">@_lead</h1>

    @* WHAT IT IS DOING, NOT JUST THAT IT IS DOING SOMETHING. A spinner during a
       warm-up that can take seconds says only "wait"; the step's name says what
       is being waited for, and turns a hang into a report. *@
    @if (_warming is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <div class="loading-step">@_warming</div>
    }

    @if (_census is { Rows.Count: > 0 })
    {
        <div class="loading-census">
            <div class="loading-summary">@_census.Summary</div>

            @foreach (var row in _census.Rows)
            {
                <div class="loading-row @(row.Present ? "loading-here" : "loading-coming")">
                    <span class="loading-mark-cell">@(row.Present ? "✓" : "↓")</span>
                    <span class="loading-title">@row.Title</span>
                    <span class="loading-detail">
                        @row.Says
                    </span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @if (_progress is { } p && p.Count > 0)
    {
        @* THE WORK, HAPPENING. A bar only while something is actually being
           fetched, so a phone that has everything shows a clean census and no
           bar rather than a full one that reads as stuck. *@
        <div class="loading-bar"><span style="width:@($"{p.Fraction * 100:0}%")"></span></div>
        @* ONE FORMATTER, ON THE RECORD. This screen and Home both show the same
           run now, and Describe is also the only thing that notices the Checking
           phase - which this page used to render as a frozen countdown. *@
        <p class="loading-step">@p.Describe()</p>
    }

    @*
        THE ONE THING WORTH DOING WHILE IT DOWNLOADS, ON THE SCREEN THAT IS UP.

        This screen covers the first part of the first-run download and hands over
        the moment the phone can talk, so Home's tour - which is the same tour -
        only appears after that. On the P30's fresh install on 2026-10-23 nothing
        asked for Circle AI's microphone at all, and the wake word, on by default,
        could not hear a thing.

        WHY NOTHING ASKED, CORRECTED. The first note here blamed "the brain landing
        last", and the code says otherwise: the plan is ordered voice, ears, brain
        precisely so the handover is early. What actually held this screen up for
        the whole download was that readiness was only re-read when the step INDEX
        changed - see the progress callback - so the moment the ears landed was the
        moment least likely to be noticed.

        ONLY STEPS THAT ACT HERE. A link would leave mid-download and skip the
        warm-up on the way out; those steps belong to Home's tour.
    *@
    @foreach (var step in _steps)
    {
        <div class="tour">
            <div class="tour-title">@step.Title</div>
            <div class="tour-body">@step.Body</div>
            <button class="tour-action" disabled="@_acting"
                    @onclick="() => Act(step.Route!)">@step.Action</button>
        </div>
    }
    @if (_acted is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="loading-step">@_acted</p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    Census? _census;
    SetupProgressReport? _progress;
    string _lead = "Waking up";

    /// <summary>What the warm-up is opening right now, or null once it is done.</summary>
    string? _warming;
    bool _left;
    bool _started;

    /// <summary>When the census first painted, so every exit holds equally long.</summary>
    DateTimeOffset _shownAt;

    /// <summary>When readiness was last asked whether the phone can talk yet.</summary>
    DateTimeOffset _askedTalkable;

    /// <summary>How often that question is worth asking. See the progress callback.</summary>
    static readonly TimeSpan TalkableEvery = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

    // HOW LONG THE CENSUS IS ON SCREEN, from first paint. Long enough to read a
    // short list and take it in - Aether's honesty screen is meant to be sat on,
    // not glimpsed. The download path overrides this: it stays until the fetch
    // is done however long that is.
    const int ReadableMs = 2600;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // OFFLINE, AND FAST. The census reads an embedded catalogue and checks
        // which anchor files are on disk by size - no network, no hashing. It
        // used to hash 470 MB of chat model on every launch, which is where the
        // four seconds went; it is milliseconds now. So no ceiling, no fallback
        // to a blank "Waking up": the census is the screen, and it shows.
        // The census is offline and fast - read it now so it is on screen the
        // instant the WebView paints, rather than popping in after.
        try { _lead = (await Wizard.ReadinessAsync()).Headline; }
        catch { /* keep "Waking up" */ }

        try { _census = await Wizard.CensusAsync(); }
        catch { _census = null; }

        // The census is ready. Show it, then release the paint handler that is
        // waiting to time the readable hold.
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        _censusReady.TrySetResult();
    }

    readonly TaskCompletionSource _censusReady = new();

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender || _started) return;
        _started = true;

        // WAIT FOR THE CENSUS, THEN START THE CLOCK. This fires the instant the
        // first frame paints, which is before OnInitializedAsync's awaits
        // resolve - so timing the hold from here read _census while it was still
        // null. The hold only means anything once there is something to hold.
        await _censusReady.Task;

        var shownAt = _shownAt = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

        // Nothing missing: a receipt. Hold it long enough to read, then warm.
        if (_census is null || _census is { Total: > 0 } c && c.Present >= c.Total)
        {
            // AND A PHONE THAT ALREADY HOLDS EVERYTHING IS STILL A PHONE WHOSE
            // SERVICE MAY NEVER HAVE BEEN ASKED FOR THE MICROPHONE.
            //
            // The ask lived only on the download branch below, so the phones it never
            // reached were exactly the ones that need it most: any phone updated in
            // place - which is what `adb install -r` does, and what the deploy notes
            // prescribe because it keeps the model store - arrives here with a
            // complete census, leaves after two and a half seconds, and stays deaf.
            // So does any phone where the first run's quiet ask was declined or
            // dismissed, and any phone set up under an earlier pair.
            //
            // AWAITED, unlike the download branch's. This exit leaves in about 2.6 s
            // and would otherwise take the card and the question with it. It costs
            // nothing on every later launch: the question is asked once in an
            // installation's life (OfferedOnce), and an empty tour from a linked
            // service returns at once.
            await OfferTourAsync();

            await HoldAtLeast(shownAt, ReadableMs);
            await LeaveAsync();
            return;
        }

        // Something missing: fetch it behind the screen, census showing what is
        // here and what is coming.
        await FetchAsync(shownAt);
    }

    /// <summary>The tour steps that act on this screen. Empty until a part lands.</summary>
    IReadOnlyList<TourStep> _steps = [];

    /// <summary>Whether a step is doing its thing, so it cannot be pressed twice.</summary>
    bool _acting;

    /// <summary>What the last step did, in the person's words.</summary>
    string? _acted;

    /// <summary>
    /// The tour for the wait, asked when the download starts - and the microphone
    /// asked for once, on the person's behalf.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// AT THE START, NOT WHEN A PART LANDS. It was asked when a progress report moved
    /// to a new item with time left on it, as Home does, and on the P30's fresh
    /// install on 2026-10-23 that moment never came: the step that asks for the
    /// microphone never reached this screen, and the wake word stayed deaf.
    /// <para>
    /// ASKED AGAIN UNTIL IT ANSWERS, because the first ask can come before the link
    /// to the service is up, and the answer then is "approve the link", a place to go,
    /// which this screen does not offer.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// THEN THE MICROPHONE, WITHOUT A PRESS. The wake word is on by default, and an
    /// "on" that waits for somebody to find a button is not on. So the screen says
    /// why and the phone asks - its own Allow is the only question. A no leaves the
    /// button for a second try; nothing is asked again on its own.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task OfferTourAsync()
    {
        for (var i = 0; i < 20 && !_left && _steps.Count == 0; i++)
        {
            var left = _progress?.Remaining is { } r && r > TimeSpan.Zero ? r : TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2);
            await TourAsync(left);
            if (_steps.Count > 0 || _left) break;

            // AN EMPTY TOUR FROM A LINKED SERVICE IS THE ANSWER, NOT A MISS.
            //
            // The loop's only exit was "a step appeared", so a phone with nothing to
            // offer - the microphone already allowed, which is most phones after the
            // first run - probed for a full minute and said nothing about it. On the
            // receipt branch above, which awaits this, that minute would have been
            // spent holding somebody on a loading screen.
            //
            // The retry exists for ONE case: the first answer can arrive before the
            // link to the service is up, and it is then "approve the link", a place to
            // go, which this screen does not offer. Once the link IS up, an empty list
            // is final.
            if (await LinkedAsync())
            {
                if (_steps.Count == 0)
                    Console.WriteLine("CIRCLEAI-LOADING tour: nothing to offer, and the service is linked");
                break;
            }

            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3));
        }

        if (_left || !_steps.Any(s => s.Route == MicrophoneAsk.Route)) return;

        _acting = true;
        _acted = MicrophoneAsk.Announce;
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        try
        {
            // THREE ANSWERS. Null is "nothing was answered" - this head cannot ask,
            // the question has already been asked once in this installation's life, or
            // the two-minute wait gave out with the dialog still on screen. It used to
            // come back false and print "Not allowed. It can still be typed to." under
            // a question the person had not answered yet.
            var allowed = await Wizard.OfferMicrophoneAsync();
            if (_left) return;

            _acted = allowed switch
            {
                true  => MicrophoneAsk.Allowed,
                false => MicrophoneAsk.NotAllowed,
                null  => null,
            };

            // AND THE CARD GOES WHEN THERE IS NOTHING LEFT TO ASK FOR. It stayed under
            // "It can hear you now.", still offering its button, for the rest of setup.
            if (allowed == true) _steps = _steps.Where(s => s.Route != MicrophoneAsk.Route).ToList();
        }
        catch
        {
            // The button is still there; a failed ask is not a reason to stop a download.
            if (!_left) _acted = null;
        }
        finally
        {
            _acting = false;
            if (!_left) await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the service is installed and this app may use it.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Never throws: a question that cannot be answered means "keep trying", which is
    /// the behaviour the retry loop had before it could ask at all.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task<bool> LinkedAsync()
    {
        try { return await Wizard.LinkApprovedAsync(); }
        catch { return false; }
    }

    /// <summary>Ask for the tour, keeping only what can be done without leaving.</summary>
    async Task TourAsync(TimeSpan left)
    {
        try
        {
            var all = await Wizard.TourAsync(left);
            if (_left) return;
            _steps = all.Where(s => s.Action is { Length: > 0 } && TourActions.ActsHere(s.Route)).ToList();
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
        catch
        {
            // A tour that cannot be built is not a reason to stop a download.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Do what a "#" step names, here. The mapping is the product's, shared with Home and Setup.</summary>
    async Task Act(string route)
    {
        if (_acting) return;
        _acting = true;
        try
        {
            var said = await TourActions.RunAsync(Wizard, route);

            // The handover may have happened while the dialog was up.
            if (_left) return;
            _acted = said;

            // AND THE STEP GOES ONCE IT IS DONE. A pressed yes left "Allow the
            // microphone" on screen, enabled, under the sentence saying it had worked.
            if (route == MicrophoneAsk.Route && said == MicrophoneAsk.Allowed)
                _steps = _steps.Where(s => s.Route != MicrophoneAsk.Route).ToList();
        }
        finally { _acting = false; }
    }

    /// <summary>Re-read the census and repaint, so a just-completed row turns green.</summary>
    async Task RefreshCensusAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var fresh = await Wizard.CensusAsync();
            if (_left) return;
            _census = fresh;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
        catch { /* keep the last census; a failed re-read is not worth a blank screen */ }
    }

    /// <summary>Hold the screen until at least this many ms have passed since it showed.</summary>
    static async Task HoldAtLeast(DateTimeOffset shownAt, int ms)
    {
        var left = ms - (int)(DateTimeOffset.UtcNow - shownAt).TotalMilliseconds;
        if (left > 0) await Task.Delay(left);
    }

    async Task FetchAsync(DateTimeOffset shownAt)
    {
        // THE ROWS TURN GREEN AS THEY LAND, not all at once at the end. The
        // report's Index says which item is downloading, so everything before it
        // is done - and when the index moves on, a row just completed and the
        // census is worth re-reading so its tick flips. Without this the screen
        // showed 0 OF 5 with a moving bar the whole way through, and the "watch
        // it fill in" that is the point of the screen never happened.
        var lastIndex = -1;
        var progress = new Progress<SetupProgressReport>(report =>
        {
            // THE RUN OUTLIVES THIS PAGE NOW. Once the handover has happened the
            // component is disposed and StateHasChanged on it throws - which,
            // on the server head, takes the circuit down rather than the page.
            if (_left) return;

            _progress = report;

            if (report.Index != lastIndex)
            {
                lastIndex = report.Index;
                // An item finished; re-census off the UI thread, then repaint.
                _ = RefreshCensusAsync();
            }

            // AND "CAN IT TALK YET" IS ASKED ON EVERY REPORT, NOT ONLY WHEN THE INDEX
            // MOVES.
            //
            // It was asked only on an index change, and the comment above this screen
            // blamed the long first run on "the brain landing last". That is not what
            // the code does: the plan puts the voice and the ears FIRST precisely so
            // this screen can hand over early, and the readiness that decides it was
            // consulted only at a step boundary. A step boundary is also the one
            // moment the ANSWER is least likely to have changed - the bytes of the
            // step that just finished are what change it, and they land before the
            // index moves.
            //
            // Throttled rather than free, because a report arrives several times a
            // second and this reads the service across a link.
            if (DateTimeOffset.UtcNow - _askedTalkable > TalkableEvery)
            {
                _askedTalkable = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
                _ = HandOverIfTalkableAsync();
            }

            InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });

        // Something to do with the wait, from the start of it. Not awaited: the run
        // is the thing this screen waits for.
        _ = OfferTourAsync();

        try { await Wizard.RunAsync(progress); }
        catch { /* a failed fetch leaves the census truthful: it is still missing */ }

        // Somebody is already on Home watching the rest of this arrive.
        if (_left) return;

        // The download is done, whatever it managed to get. Re-read the census
        // so the last tick is current, then go.
        await RefreshCensusAsync();

        // Let the finished census sit a moment - the ticks turning green is the
        // last thing worth seeing before Home.
        await HoldAtLeast(shownAt, ReadableMs);
        await LeaveAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Leave the moment the phone can hold a conversation.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// CanTalk, NOT Stage. Waiting for ReadyStage.Ready would wait for the
    /// BRAIN, which is most of the download and none of the reason somebody can
    /// start using the phone. CanTalk is the part that tracks what has actually
    /// landed - voice present AND ears present - and it is true from the moment
    /// those two arrive, which the plan orders first on purpose.
    /// <para>
    /// It is also the one field every head answers the same way. The stage a
    /// running setup reports is a judgement about wording; CanTalk is a fact
    /// about bytes.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The same readable hold as every other exit. Handing over the instant a
    /// row ticks would make the census a flash on a fast connection, and the
    /// census is the screen.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task HandOverIfTalkableAsync()
    {
        if (_left) return;

        try
        {
            var now = await Wizard.ReadinessAsync();
            if (!now.CanTalk || _left) return;

            await HoldAtLeast(_shownAt, ReadableMs);
            await LeaveAsync();
        }
        catch
        {
            // A readiness read that fails is not a reason to hold somebody on a
            // loading screen; the fetch carries on and the normal exit still runs.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Do not leave this screen until the app is actually ready to be used.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// DOWNLOADED IS NOT READY, AND THIS SCREEN USED TO STOP AT DOWNLOADED. The
    /// census counts bytes on disk, so the gate opened while the transcriber had
    /// never been opened, the voice had never been built, and the phonemizer
    /// might not be wired at all.
    /// <para>
    /// HOW MUCH THIS BUYS, HONESTLY: the first spoken reply logged "(INCLUDING
    /// model open)" and no longer does, which is about two seconds. It does NOT
    /// fix a slow first answer - this note used to claim the eleven-second first
    /// decode and that was wrong, as the logs it cited already showed: every stt
    /// line reports built=15..53 ms against decode=11648..29541 ms. Opening is
    /// milliseconds; the decode is Whisper on a P30 and belongs to the model.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// So the cost landed on the first turn - the one where somebody decides
    /// whether this thing works - after a screen had already told them it was
    /// ready. This screen exists to absorb exactly that wait, and it was handing
    /// it to the user instead.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Never fatal. A warm-up that fails is reported and the app still opens: a
    /// phone that will not start is worse than a phone whose first turn is slow,
    /// and the wiring report says which it is.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task WarmAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            // The hooks first: they are milliseconds, and a null phonemizer means
            // the voice cannot be warmed at all - better to say so than to open a
            // model for a voice that will refuse.
            var hooks = await Probe.HooksAsync();
            foreach (var row in hooks.Failing)
            {
                _warming = row.Title;
                await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            }

            var progress = new Progress<string>(line =>
            {
                _warming = line;
                InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            });

            await Talk.PrepareAsync(progress);

            // AND THE BRAIN, WHICH WAS NEVER WARMED HERE AT ALL.
            //
            // This screen warmed the voice and left, so Home arrived with a cold
            // model and the first question came back "brain warming up, try again
            // shortly". On a P30 that load is thirteen to twenty-three seconds; a 2B
            // on a Tensor G2 took nineteen. All of it was spent AFTER the person was
            // told the phone was ready.
            //
            // Asking is starting - StateAsync binds the service with AutoCreate - so
            // the first call does the work and the rest watch it happen.
            await BrainAsync();
        }
        catch
        {
            // Reported by the probe's own log; never a reason to trap somebody
            // on a loading screen.
        }
        finally
        {
            _warming = null;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The only way off this screen, and it warms on the way out.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// WARMING USED TO LIVE AT ONE OF THE TWO EXITS. The "nothing missing" path
    /// warmed and left; the path that had just DOWNLOADED something left without
    /// warming - so the person who waited through a setup, and is most likely to
    /// speak the moment it finishes, got the cold eleven-second first decode.
    /// The one that had nothing to download got the warm one.
    /// <para>
    /// Putting it behind the door rather than in front of each exit means a third
    /// exit cannot forget: leaving without warming is no longer something this
    /// file can express. The _left guard makes it once, however many callers.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task LeaveAsync()
    {
        if (_left) return;
        _left = true;

        await WarmAsync();
        Nav.NavigateTo("home");
    }

    /// <summary>Wait for the model, but never for ever.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A CEILING, NOT A TARGET. The whole philosophy of this screen is that it gets
    /// out of the way - it already refuses to hold somebody for the last byte of a
    /// download, and it must not start holding them for a model that will not load.
    /// Forty seconds is roughly twice the slowest load measured (nineteen for a 2B
    /// on a Tensor G2, thirteen to twenty-three for a 0.8B on a P30); past that,
    /// something is wrong and Home says so better than a spinner does.
    ///
    /// SAYING WHICH STAGE, because a screen that sits still for twenty seconds under
    /// one unchanging word is indistinguishable from one that has hung.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task BrainAsync()
    {
        var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(40);

        while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
        {
            BrainState state;
            try { state = await Brain.StateAsync().ConfigureAwait(false); }
            catch { return; }   // no service, no brain - Home explains it properly

            if (state.Ready) return;

            // ITS OWN WORDS, NOT OURS. The service says "loading the model" or
            // "no model on this device yet - set it up first", and inventing a
            // cheerier sentence here would hide the second one.
            _warming = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(state.Detail) ? "Starting the brain" : state.Detail;

            // NOT AWAITED, LIKE THE PROGRESS CALLBACK ABOVE IT. This runs inside
            // OnInitializedAsync, which the renderer is already awaiting - awaiting a
            // render from in here deadlocks, and the first version of this loop did:
            // the screen never left and the test sat out its whole twenty seconds.
            _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

            // OFF THE RENDERER'S CONTEXT. This waits on a real timer from inside
            // OnInitializedAsync, which the renderer is awaiting - resuming on that
            // context means the continuation queues behind the very render it is
            // waiting for, and nothing moves. The UI update above goes through
            // InvokeAsync precisely so this does not have to.
            await Task.Delay(500).ConfigureAwait(false);
        }
    }

    // THE LOCAL FORMATTER IS GONE, and it was the wrong base. This one was binary,
    // so the same 22 797 996 902-byte bundle read "21.2 GB" here and "22.8 GB" on
    // the setup screen. CensusRow.Size is decimal, which is the unit the catalogue
    // is persisted in and therefore the unit the fit rule reasoned about.

    public void Dispose() => _left = true;
}
